    {
      "name": "Health, science & medicine",
      "items": [
        {
          "headline": "JAMA Network Open: GPT-4o mini discharge summaries signed off at 92.2% against 70.0% for clinician-written ones",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "JAMA Network Open", "url": "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2856756" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "In a retrospective comparative effectiveness study with a prespecified noninferiority framework, the adjusted probability of a reviewer signing off with no or minor edits was 92.2% (95% CI, 88.2%-94.9%) for summaries generated by GPT-4o mini against 70.0% (95% CI, 56.6%-80.7%) for clinician-authored summaries, an adjusted risk difference of 22.2 percentage points (95% CI, 19.0 to 27.5) against a prespecified noninferiority margin of −5 percentage points.",
            "The study covered admissions to five hospitals in North West London from September 2022 to September 2023, with 1,294 unique cases split 649 clinician-authored and 645 model-generated, reviewed by 12 resident physicians. The model was GPT-4o mini, the 18 July 2024 release, run through a clinician-codesigned prompt pipeline.",
            "This is a peer-reviewed clinical comparison on a real documentation task rather than a benchmark, and the comparator did not do well either: only 70.0% of clinician-written summaries were signed off with no or minor edits.",
            "Errors did not disappear. Hallucinations appeared in 6.0% of model summaries (43 of 720) against 6.9% of clinician summaries (50 of 722), and the authors conclude the model summaries met the noninferiority standard but require close clinician oversight because omissions and hallucinations persisted. The study is retrospective, not a trial of deployed use, and used a model released in 2024."
          ],
          "topics": ["healthcare", "openai", "evals", "labor", "incidents"],
          "impact": "mixed"
        },
        {
          "headline": "Isomorphic Labs raises a $5 billion Series C co-led by Greenoaks and Sequoia for AI drug design",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "CNBC", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/17/isomorphic-labs-series-c-ai-drug-discovery-.html" },
            { "name": "The Next Web", "url": "https://thenextweb.com/news/isomorphic-labs-5bn-series-c-sequoia-greenoaks" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Isomorphic Labs, the AI drug discovery company founded by Demis Hassabis, said on Tuesday it had raised a $5 billion Series C co-led by Greenoaks and Sequoia Capital, alongside Andreessen Horowitz and with investment from Alphabet. Existing investors Thrive Capital, MGX, GV, CapitalG, Temasek and the UK Sovereign AI Fund also took part. The company last raised a $2.1 billion Series B in May.",
            "Hassabis said: \"This round enables us to accelerate our progress: building foundational biology models, deepening our systemic disease understanding, and driving our first therapeutic programs towards clinical trials to deliver tangible impact for patients.\" The Next Web reports more than 20 programmes underway.",
            "CNBC reports drug discovery startups have raised $23.2 billion in 2026, a record, according to Dealroom, and places the round three and a half months after Hassabis stepped back as CEO of Google DeepMind.",
            "Isomorphic did not disclose a valuation; The Next Web notes Bloomberg reported earlier that the round would value the company at about $50 billion, which neither report confirms. On timing, The Next Web says Hassabis said in January the company expected its first trials by the end of 2026 but gave no updated target, saying it is on track with regulatory submissions and will have more to announce in 2027 — so no clinical result has been reported. The announcement broke shortly before this edition's window opened and was not in the 17 November edition."
          ],
